  "title": "Spectroscopic and Magnetic Properties of Coordination Compounds Summary",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Crystal field theory treats interactions between the electrons on the metal and the ligands as a simple electrostatic effect. The presence of the ligands near the metal ion changes the energies of the metal "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "d"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals relative to their energies in the free ion. Both the color and the magnetic properties of a complex can be attributed to this crystal field splitting. The magnitude of the splitting (Δ"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "oct"
              },
              {
                "text": ") depends on the nature of the ligands bonded to the metal. Strong-field ligands produce large splitting and favor low-spin complexes, in which the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "sub": true,
                "text": "g"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals are completely filled before any electrons occupy the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "e"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "sub": true,
                "text": "g"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals. Weak-field ligands favor formation of high-spin complexes. The "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "sub": true,
                "text": "g"
              },
              {
                "text": " and the "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "e"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "sub": true,
                "text": "g"
              },
              {
                "text": " orbitals are singly occupied before any are doubly occupied."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3838833972",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
